Bruce Zimov writes:

> Well so far I think the 2 positions here are:
> 
> 1) Take down the system to get the technology here quicker.
> 
> and 
> 
> 2)  Work with the system to get the technology here quicker, because
>     the system just ain't coming down.

If by working with "the system" you mean--as you seem to--working to
get government funding, a la your point about lobbying Clinton to get
it in her health plan, then you've left out a third position:

3) Let like-minded folks pursue cryonics, trade, and privacy with as
little involvement with the government as possible.

You may say this third approach is logically equivalent to #1, in
terms of "sneaking around" and being subversive, but I don't think so.
The notion I favor, "crypto anarchy," does not necessarily mean a
total destruction of the State, however desirable that may be, but an
expansion of channels of interaction and trade that are beyond the
power of the State to practically control.

Things like this List, with members on all continents and with
effectively instantaneous communication and mutual aid (to some
degree). are a step in this direction. Governments are largely
powerless to control these virtual communities.

Cryonics is of course in a much more precarious state, dealing as it
does (literally) with the corpus delicti, so to speak.

Even for cryonics, I thin #3 is the best tack--interact with the State
in minimal ways, just get them to agree not to force certain burial
practices and then don't interact with them further. Inviting them to
fund, regulate, administer, or otherwise deal with cryonics is
inviting them to shut it all down on a whim (or when the voters decide
its "icky").

Why call them back from heaven?

--Tim May


