Date: Thu, 30 Dec 93 19:50:21 EST
From: Andy Wilson <ajw@Think.COM>
Subject: the extropian pan-opticon

   Date: Thu, 30 Dec 1993 12:24:09 -0800
   From: Brian D Williams <talon57@well.sf.ca.us>

...

Prisons exist to prevent those incarcerated from committing further
crimes.  I don't subscribe to vengeance being a desirable goal.  All
I care about is that me and the people I care about are protected from
criminals.

   Is it possible to have economically sound prisons, where the cost
   of incarceration could be at least partially recouped? 

Prisons should be private, for profit institutions.  When someone
is sentenced,  they choose which prison they want to go to.  So
prisons are competing for inmates.  Yet they must remain profitable,
or go out of business.  They should cost the "taxpayers" diddly.
There is nothing that entitles prisoners to free room and board for
committing a crime.

Inmates would be responsible for paying their way,  and any additional
wealth they create would be theirs, modulo any compensation they need
to make to their victims in the case of property theft or damage.  I'm
not sure whether survivors of murder victims should get any money, that
should be taken care of by insurance.  They should also have to pay for
the costs of their arrest and prosecution.

Inmates who fail to do the minimum required work to pay their way would,
you guessed it, starve.

   Should prisoners be isolated in single cells to prevent spreading 
   of bad memes?

Nope.  The only reason to isolate prisoners is if they present a danger
to other inmates.

   Should they have telephones? Internet access?  Even more extensive 
   libraries? 

This would all have to be determined by market forces between competing
prisons, but in general...

Yep.  They should be able to tele-commute as long as they pay their
prison "rent" and damages to their victims.  The only restriction is
that it should be clear to anyone they have virtual contact with that
they are in prison,  and what they did to get there.  No anonymous
remailers for them.  This could be done by kludging their mail software,
which some of them would crack,  but hey, that would give somebody a
fun job, to outwit the inmate hackers.

The prison would be liable for any fraud committed over the net by
inmates,  and would therefore restrict net access as they see fit.
Prisoners can always switch prisons.

If a prisoner escapes, the prison has to pay for their re-capture,
and if that fails, it has to pay their victim compensation.  This can
be handled as a contract between the victim or survivors and the
prison itself.

   Should conjugal visits be allowed? 

Most emphatically yes,  and prisons should not be gender-segregated 
either.

   Why is it society refuses to discuss the extensive homosexual conduct that
   goes on? (this is not meant to offend anyones sexual choice, but
   refers to choices made under these conditions) 

This is a good question.  Any damn fool can tell that humans were not
designed to be gender-segregated.  Look at the history of English boarding
schools.  No one knows how many more crimes are the result of these
barbaric conditions.

Prison rapists should be isolated.  There is no excuse for tolerance of
this behavior.

   Is rehabilitation possible?

It would be if prisoners would have to demonstrate it before they are
let out.  If they are not rehabilatated and they have committed a
sufficiently serious violent crime,  then they should remain in prison.

   Desirable? 

Yep.

   Extropic minds want to know.

Whew.  I guess maybe I should write a real essay on this sometime.

Andy


